Where the protocol comes from
-----------------------------

This program speaks to the same hardware as ykurcmd, the control application
Yepkit publishes for their YKUR relay board:

    https://github.com/Yepkit/ykurcmd
    Copyright (c) 2016 Yepkit Lda (its LICENSE.md)

The protocol this program speaks was implemented from Yepkit's YKUR datasheet,
rev. 1.2.1, listed in doc/README.md, and then checked against a board, where
firmware 1.2.1 answers some commands differently from the datasheet (see the
README). It was not derived from the source of ykurcmd. That source was read
afterwards, only to describe how the two programs behave differently. What the
two share is the protocol, which both must speak because the hardware requires
it, and the letters of the command line switches. Facts and interfaces of that
kind are not what copyright protects, so this notice is here because the work
deserves crediting, not because a license compels it.

Nothing was taken from that source: no source file, no line of the help text
and no message the program prints. Every string literal of ykurcmd (its .cpp
and .h files, without the bundled hidapi) was compared with every string
literal of this program, escapes resolved and case ignored. None is shared.
The longest common pieces are terms of the hardware - "serial number" and
"firmware version", with a neighbouring word at most 20 characters.

Yepkit Lda has neither endorsed nor reviewed this work and does not support it.
Their trademarks and product names are used only to say which hardware this
program drives.

The behaviour differs from the original; the table "Where it differs from
ykurcmd" in the README lists how.


Third party components
----------------------

No third party C library is vendored or linked. The program reaches the device
through Apple's IOKit framework on macOS and through the kernel's hidraw
interface on Linux, from Rust.

Every crate that goes into the binary is under MIT or Apache-2.0 terms; they
are listed with their licenses in THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md.
